WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.023
Book Four, Proposition Ten.

2
00:00:02.023 --> 00:00:08.020
To construct an isosceles triangle having each of the angles at the base double of the remaining one.

3
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:18.410
Let any straight line A B be set out; it is required to construct on A B an isosceles triangle A B D having each of the angles at the base B D double of the vertex angle at A.

4
00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:29.422
By Two Eleven, cut A B at C in golden section so that the rectangle contained by A B and B C equals the square on C A.

5
00:00:29.422 --> 00:00:42.079
Describe circle A B—shown as B D E—with center A and defining radius point B, and by Four One fit into the circle the chord B D equal to A C.

6
00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:49.440
Join A D and D C, and by Four Five circumscribe circle A C D about triangle A C D.

7
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:58.441
Because rectangle A B, B C equals the square on A C, and A C equals B D, rectangle A B, B C equals the square on B D.

8
00:00:58.441 --> 00:01:10.689
Therefore by Three Thirty-Seven, B D is tangent to circle A C D at D, and by Three Thirty-Two the tangent-chord angle B D C equals the alternate inscribed angle D A C.

9
00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:21.323
Add angle C D A to each: the whole angle B D A equals the sum of angles C D A and D A C, which by One Thirty-Two equals the exterior angle B C D.

10
00:01:21.323 --> 00:01:33.131
And because radii A D and A B are equal, by One Five base angles B D A and D B A are equal, making all three angles B D A, D B A, and B C D equal to one another.

11
00:01:33.131 --> 00:01:39.952
In triangle B C D, equal angles at B and C give D C equal to B D, and therefore equal to C A.

12
00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:48.239
Hence in isosceles triangle A C D, angle C D A equals angle D A C, so exterior angle B C D is double angle D A C.

13
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:58.140
Therefore in isosceles triangle A B D, each of the base angles B D A and D B A is double the vertex angle D A B, as was required to do.

14
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:07.565
Proposition Four Ten is the architectural hinge of Book Four: the construction of the golden thirty-six, seventy-two, seventy-two degree triangle.

15
00:02:07.565 --> 00:02:22.614
As Heath details, this triangle arises directly from the Pythagorean pentagram—their badge of recognition called Health—and bridges Book Two's golden section in Two Eleven with Book Three's tangent-secant power of a point in Three Thirty-Seven.

16
00:02:22.614 --> 00:02:34.456
Because the vertex angle is thirty-six degrees, its bisection divides the right angle into five equal parts, while chord B D is already the side of a regular decagon inscribed in circle B D E.

17
00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:49.320
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-two unites seventeen direct prerequisites across ninety-five ancestor nodes, powering the regular pentagon, decagon, fifteen-gon, and the cosmic dodecahedron and icosahedron of Book Thirteen.

18
00:02:49.740 --> 00:03:02.946
